---
title: "Tutorial 1: Build export presets"
sidebar:
  label: "1. Build export presets"
  order: 1
---

In this tutorial you build a tabular export preset that turns NAHPU mammal
specimen records into a CSV file for the
[Specify](https://www.specifysoftware.org/) WorkBench of the LSU Museum of
Natural Science (LSUMZ) Mammalogy collection. The target is the collection's
incoming-data sheet: one row per specimen, with each part written as a
numbered preparation (`PrepType1`, `PrepType1Count`, `PrepType1Location`,
`PrepType2`, …).

You start from an empty preset and add one kind of column at a time: plain
fields, combined and blank columns, formatted values, and the specimen's parts.
By the end you will have a preset named `LSUMZ-mammalogy`, a test export you
have checked against your records, and a file the LSUMZ collection manager can
map in the WorkBench.

{% aside type="note" %}
Every Specify database is configured differently. The column names here follow
the LSUMZ Mammalogy WorkBench sheet. For another collection, ask its collection
manager for their sheet and use their names wherever they differ. NAHPU also
ships the finished `LSUMZ-mammalogy` preset for mammalogy projects, under
`Setup NAHPU` or `Load defaults`. If you have already loaded it, give yours
another name, such as `LSUMZ-mammalogy-mine`, and compare the two.
{% /aside %}

## Before you start

You need:

- A NAHPU project with a few complete mammal specimen records. Include at least
  one specimen with several parts, such as a skin, a skull, and a tissue with a
  freezer box position, so you can see how repeated values export.
- The LSUMZ Mammalogy incoming-data sheet, or another collection's column list.
- About 30 minutes.

## 1. Plan the columns

Write down the columns before you open the editor. The WorkBench maps columns
by name, so match the sheet's spelling exactly, including spaces and case.

| Column | NAHPU source | How it is built |
|---|---|---|
| `Catalog Number` | `specimen::museumID` | Single field |
| `Class`, `Order`, `Family`, `Genus`, `Species` | `taxonomy::taxonClass`, `taxonOrder`, `taxonFamily`, `genus`, `specificEpithet` | Single fields |
| `Sex` | `mammalAttribute::sex` | Label instead of code |
| `Continent` | — | Blank column |
| `Country`, `State`, `County` | `geography::country`, `stateProvince`, `county` | Single fields |
| `Locality Name` | `geography::municipality` + `geography::locality` | List joined with `; ` |
| `Elevation` | `coordinate::elevationInMeter` | Single field |
| `Elevation Unit` | — | `m` when an elevation is recorded |
| `Collector1LastName` | `personnel::name` | Rewritten as `Last, First` |
| `Field Number` | `specimen::fieldNumber` | Single field |
| `Start Date` | `specimen::collectionDate` | Date as `dd/mm/yyyy` |
| `PrepType1`, `PrepType1Count`, `PrepType1Location`, … | `specimenPart::type`, `count`, `storageLocation` | Numbered part columns |
| `Remarks` | `mammalAttribute::remark` | Single field |
| `Latitude`, `Longitude` | `coordinate::decimalLatitude`, `decimalLongitude` | Single fields |
| `MaxErrorDistance` | `coordinate::uncertaintyInMeters` | Single field |
| `OtherCollection OtherCollNumber` | — | Blank column |
| `Total Length`, `TailLength`, `HFLength`, `Ear`, `Weight` | `mammalAttribute::totalLength`, `tailLength`, `hindFootLength`, `earLength`, `weight` | Single fields |

NAHPU has no continent or other-collection number, so those two columns are
written blank for the collection to fill in. Keeping them means the file still
matches the WorkBench sheet column for column.

## 2. Create the preset

{% screenshots
   large="app-screenshots/tutorials/specify-preset-large.webp"
   largeAlt="NAHPU Tabular Export Presets screen on a tablet, editing the LSUMZ-mammalogy preset"
   small="app-screenshots/tutorials/specify-preset-small.webp"
   smallAlt="NAHPU Tabular Export Presets list on a phone"
   small2="app-screenshots/tutorials/specify-preset-small-2.webp"
   small2Alt="NAHPU Edit Preset tab on a phone, editing the LSUMZ-mammalogy preset" %}
{% pin id="A" label="New preset" large=[94, 3.3] small=[82.7, 3.1] /%}
{% pin id="B" label="Update name and description" large=[92.3, 46.6] small2=[82, 47] /%}
{% pin id="C" label="Record type" large=[94.9, 56.3] small2=[87.4, 55.8] /%}
{% pin id="D" label="Specimen taxon group" large=[94.9, 63.9] small2=[87.4, 62.8] /%}
{% pin id="E" label="Generated header format" large=[94.9, 71.6] small2=[87.4, 69.8] /%}
{% pin id="F" label="Edit Fields" large=[73.3, 82.1] small2=[45, 79.5] /%}
{% pin id="G" label="Preview export table" large=[94.9, 82.1] small2=[87.4, 79.5] /%}
{% /screenshots %}

{% steps %}

1. Open `Settings` > `Tabular Data`.
2. Select the add button {% ref id="A" /%}, enter `LSUMZ-mammalogy`, and
   create it. Names may use letters, numbers, underscores, and dashes. On a
   phone, the new preset opens in the `Edit Preset` tab.
3. Set `Record type` {% ref id="C" /%} to `Specimen`. Each specimen becomes one
   row.
4. Set `Specimen taxon group` {% ref id="D" /%} to `Mammals`.
5. Set `Generated header format` {% ref id="E" /%} to `fieldName`. You will
   name every column yourself, so this only matters for a column you forget to
   name.
6. Type a description, for example `LSUMZ Specify WorkBench sheet: one row
   per mammal specimen.`, and select `Update` {% ref id="B" /%}.

{% /steps %}

Settings save as you change them; only the name and description wait for
`Update`. The columns themselves are set under `Edit Fields`
{% ref id="F" /%}, and the eye button {% ref id="G" /%} previews the export.
Both are used in the steps below.

{% aside type="tip" %}
Choose `Specimen` rather than `Specimen parts` here. `Specimen parts` writes one
row per part, which suits a tissue inventory. Specify expects one row per
collection object, with its preparations beside it.
{% /aside %}

## 3. Add the single fields

Most columns copy one NAHPU field unchanged.

{% screenshots
   large="app-screenshots/tutorials/specify-fields-large.webp"
   largeAlt="NAHPU Edit Preset Mappings screen on a tablet with the Geography group expanded"
   small="app-screenshots/tutorials/specify-fields-small.webp"
   smallAlt="NAHPU Available Fields tab on a phone with the Geography group expanded"
   small2="app-screenshots/tutorials/specify-fields-small-2.webp"
   small2Alt="NAHPU Selected Mappings tab on a phone" %}
{% pin id="A" label="Field group" large=[10, 23.5] small=[30, 27.6] /%}
{% pin id="B" label="Field checkbox" large=[45.5, 33.5] small=[85.5, 36.8] /%}
{% pin id="C" label="Selected mappings" large=[58, 10.9] small=[74.8, 8.6] /%}
{% pin id="D" label="Add custom field" large=[81.8, 10.9] small2=[74.8, 16.2] /%}
{% pin id="E" label="Add nested" large=[93.7, 10.9] small2=[86.7, 16.2] /%}
{% pin id="F" label="Customize" large=[88.7, 20.3] small2=[65.5, 24.7] /%}
{% pin id="G" label="Drag to reorder" large=[95.7, 20.3] small2=[85.8, 24.7] /%}
{% pin id="H" label="Preview" large=[93, 94.2] small2=[78.2, 93.7] /%}
{% /screenshots %}

{% steps %}

1. Select `Edit Fields`. The `Edit Preset Mappings` screen lists source fields
   under `Insert Source Field` and your columns under `Selected Mappings`
   {% ref id="C" /%}. On a phone, these are the `Available Fields` and
   `Selected Mappings` tabs.
2. Expand a group {% ref id="A" /%} and check {% ref id="B" /%} each field your
   plan marks as a single field: `museumID` under `Specimen`, the taxonomy
   fields under `Taxonomy`, the geography fields under `Geography`, the
   elevation, coordinates, and uncertainty under `Coordinate`, `fieldNumber`
   under `Specimen`, and `remark` and the measurements under
   `Mammal Attribute`.
3. Select the pencil {% ref id="F" /%} on each mapping and type the sheet's
   header into `Column name (optional)`, such as `Catalog Number` for
   `museumID` or `HFLength` for `hindFootLength`. Select `Done` to keep it.
4. Drag each mapping by its handle {% ref id="G" /%} into the order of your
   plan. You can do this at the end, once every column exists.

{% /steps %}

The other controls in `Selected Mappings` come next: `Add custom field`
{% ref id="D" /%} in steps 4 and 5, `Add nested` {% ref id="E" /%} in step 6,
and `Preview` {% ref id="H" /%} in step 7.

Pick each field from the group that owns it. For example, `startDate` exists
under both `Project` and `Coll Event`, and neither is the specimen's own
collection date, which is `collectionDate` under `Specimen`.

## 4. Build the composed and blank columns

Some columns are not a single field. Build them with custom fields, which use
the mapping dialog shown here: `Add field` {% ref id="B" /%}, `Add text`
{% ref id="C" /%}, and `Add conditional text` {% ref id="D" /%} build the value
one segment at a time. The same dialog handles the parts in
[step 6](#6-add-the-parts-as-numbered-preparations).

