### Locality Name

Join the municipality and the specific locality with a semicolon, skipping
whichever is empty.

{% steps %}

1. In `Selected Mappings`, select `Add custom field`. On a phone, it is the
   first icon beside the `Selected Mappings` heading.
2. Set `Mapping type` {% ref id="A" /%} to `List field`.
3. Open `Advanced` and paste this into `Raw source expression`:

   ```text
   [geography::municipality]|[geography::locality]
   ```

4. Set `List output` to `One column` and `Separator` to `Semicolon (A; B)`.
5. Enter `Locality Name` as the column name, then select `Done`.

{% /steps %}

A record exports as `Passi Timur; Mt. Ambang`, or `Passi Timur` alone when no
specific locality was recorded. See
[Export expressions](/en/usages/export/export-expressions/#full-locality) for
more ways to join geography fields.

### Elevation Unit

Write `m` only when the specimen has an elevation, so an empty elevation does
not carry a stray unit.

{% steps %}

1. Add a custom field and select `Add conditional text` {% ref id="D" /%}.
2. Set the condition to `coordinate::elevationInMeter` `Is not empty`.
3. Enter `m` as `Text when matched` and leave `Text otherwise (optional)`
   empty.
4. Check that `Expression:` {% ref id="E" /%} reads
   `[[if][coordinate::elevationInMeter!=""]=>"m"]]`.
5. Enter `Elevation Unit` as the `Column name` {% ref id="F" /%}, then select
   `Done`.

{% /steps %}

### Blank columns

{% steps %}

1. Add a custom field and leave its value empty.
2. Enter `Continent` as the `Column name` {% ref id="F" /%} and select `Done`.
   A named custom field with no segments is written as an empty column.
3. Repeat for `OtherCollection OtherCollNumber`.

{% /steps %}

## 5. Format values for Specify

Three columns hold a NAHPU value in a different shape.

### Sex

NAHPU stores sex as a code, such as `0` for male. Specify needs the word.

{% steps %}

1. Add a custom field, select `Add field` {% ref id="B" /%}, and choose
   `mammalAttribute::sex`.
2. Select `Show label` on that segment. The expression
   becomes `[mammalAttribute::sex#label]`.
3. Enter `Sex` as the column name and select `Done`.

{% /steps %}

Use `Show label` the same way for any other coded field your collection
imports, such as `reproductiveStage`. See
[Encoded field labels](/en/usages/export/export-expressions/#encoded-labels).

### Start Date

{% steps %}

1. Check `collectionDate` under `Specimen` and select its pencil.
2. Set `Value format` to `Date`.
3. Open `Advanced` and enter `dd/mm/yyyy` as the `Raw format option`.
4. Enter `Start Date` as the column name and select `Done`.

{% /steps %}

A collection date of 17 February 2016 exports as `17/02/2016`. Other options
include `yyyy-mm-dd`, `mm/dd/yyyy`, and `dd-month-yyyy`.

### Collector1LastName

NAHPU stores a person's full name, such as `Heru Handika`. The sheet wants the
last name first: `Handika, Heru`.

{% steps %}

1. Check `name` under `Personnel` and select its pencil.
2. Open `Replace text` {% ref id="G" /%} and select `Add replacement`.
3. Set `Match` to `Regex`, `Find` to `^([^,]*[^\s,])\s+([^\s,]+)$`, and
   `Replace with` to `$2, $1`.
4. Enter `Collector1LastName` as the column name and select `Done`.

{% /steps %}

The rule moves the last word to the front. A name that already contains a
comma is left as it is. Specify still matches the result against its agent
list, so check unusual names, such as two-word family names, in the preview.

## 6. Add the parts as numbered preparations

A specimen can have any number of parts. Write each part into its own group of
columns, numbered from 1, so the WorkBench can map them to Preparation #1, #2,
and so on.

{% steps %}

1. In `Selected Mappings`, select `Add nested`. On a phone, it is the second
   icon beside the `Selected Mappings` heading.
2. Set `Related records` {% ref id="H" /%} to `specimenPart`.
3. Open `Choose child fields` {% ref id="J" /%} and check `type`, `count`, and
   `storageLocation`, in that order. Checked fields appear under
   `Selected child fields` in column order.
4. Set `Nested output` to `Indexed columns`.
5. Under each child field, type its column name {% ref id="I" /%} with `{n}`
   where the part number goes:
   - `type`: `PrepType{n}`
   - `count`: `PrepType{n}Count`
   - `storageLocation`: `PrepType{n}Location`
6. Check that the `Output example` shows `PrepType1`, `PrepType1Count`,
   `PrepType1Location`, `PrepType2`, …, then select `Done`.

{% /steps %}

A specimen with a skin, skull, and tissue now fills `PrepType1` to
`PrepType3Location`, with the tissue's freezer position, such as `(127,B2)`,
in its location column. The number of groups follows the specimen with the most
parts in the export, and specimens with fewer parts leave the extra columns
blank.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Specify matches `Prep Type` values against its own list. If NAHPU part types
such as `Liver` or `Oral swab` are not in that list, the WorkBench reports them
during validation. Agree on the vocabulary with your collection manager, or use
`Replace text` {% ref id="G" /%} in the mapping to rename values on export.
{% /aside %}

The parts form one block, so a column cannot sit between two preparation
groups. Place `Remarks` before or after the block; the WorkBench maps by name,
not position.

## 7. Preview the output

{% steps %}

1. Select `Preview` at the bottom of `Selected Mappings`, or the eye button
   beside `Edit Fields` on the preset.
2. Select a few specimens, including the one with several parts.
3. Check, column by column:
   - the headers match the sheet exactly;
   - `Sex` shows `Male` or `Female`, not a code;
   - `Start Date` reads `dd/mm/yyyy` and `Collector1LastName` reads
     `Last, First`;
   - the `PrepType` groups line up with the parts on the specimen record;
   - `Continent` and `OtherCollection OtherCollNumber` are empty.

{% /steps %}

Fix anything that looks wrong in `Edit Fields`, then preview again.

## 8. Export and import into Specify

{% steps %}

1. Open the project menu and select `Export records`.
2. Choose the `LSUMZ-mammalogy` preset and `CSV` as the format.
3. Select `Export records` and open the file to confirm the row count.
4. In Specify, open the WorkBench and import the CSV as a new data set.
5. Create an upload plan with **Collection Object** as the base table, and map
   each column to its Specify field.
6. Validate the data set, fix any reported cells, and upload.

{% /steps %}

Save the upload plan in Specify. Because the preset always writes the same
headers in the same order, the next season's export maps with the same plan.

## 9. Share the preset with your team

Back in `Settings` > `Tabular Data`, open the preset's row menu and choose
`Export this preset`. Teammates import the file through `Import`, so every
device in the field produces the same columns.

## What you learned

- **Record type** decides what one row is. Use `Specimen` for one row per
  collection object.
- **Single fields** copy a value; `Column name (optional)` renames its header.
- **List fields** join several fields and skip the empty ones.
- **Conditional text** writes a value, such as a unit, only when a field is
  filled.
- **A named custom field with no value** writes a blank column.
- **`Show label`, `Date`, and `Replace text`** reshape values into what the
  receiving database expects.
- **Nested records with indexed columns** spread repeated parts across numbered
  column groups, and `{n}` in a child field's column name sets each name.

